  "response": "The dispute was not settled by argument about plausibility but by successive campaigns of field, geochemical and geochronological work on the two candidate structures themselves. The Chicxulub crater measures roughly 180 kilometres in diameter. The body that formed the structure was approximately two kilometres in diameter. Luis Alvarez and colleagues proposed the asteroid impact hypothesis in a 1980 paper in Science. The geochemistry of the boundary itself allows exactly one of two descriptions, one of which must hold while the other must fail: either the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary clay layer is enriched in iridium relative to surrounding sediments, the anomaly that led those authors to advance the hypothesis, or the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary clay layer shows no iridium enrichment relative to surrounding sediments. Shocked quartz grains occur in boundary deposits at sites on multiple continents.\n\nThe competing explanation rested on an entirely different body of rock. The Deccan Traps are a flood basalt province in west-central India, and it was this flood-basalt character that caused the release of large volumes of carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide into the atmosphere. Any reconstruction of the terminal Cretaceous climate crisis must accordingly concede at least one of two stratospheric burdens, and perhaps both of them: the volcanic gases just described, or the dust and sulfur aerosols that the impact injected into the stratosphere.\n\nChronology was the first arbiter, since a cause must stand in the right temporal relation to its effect, and the two candidate events had to be placed on a common timescale with uncertainties of tens of thousands rather than millions of years. Argon-argon and uranium-lead dating were applied to Deccan lavas and Chicxulub impact glass to compare their ages. Radiometric dating places the Chicxulub impact two million years after the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary.\n\nDrilling then supplied the decisive compositional constraint. IODP-ICDP Expedition 364 drilled into the peak ring of the Chicxulub structure in 2016, and the material it brought up indicates that thick sulfur-bearing evaporite deposits lay among the rocks struck at Chicxulub. Because the target succession beneath the crater was so charged with sulfur, the stratospheric injection of dust and aerosols described above followed as a direct consequence, and the resulting darkness and cooling were severe enough to collapse marine and terrestrial food chains within a few years. Whereas placing the Chicxulub impact two million years after the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary would divorce the crater from the extinction altogether, the sulfur-rich composition of the target rocks pointed the other way, and it was on that evidence that a 2010 review panel of 41 scientists writing in Science concluded that the Chicxulub impact rather than Deccan volcanism triggered the mass extinction.",
